---
import BaseLayout from "~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "~/lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the platform-agnostic email spec"
  description="Learn how The Email Specification turns scattered RFCs, accessibility guidance, and provider requirements into one testable, open checklist."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / About</nav>
      <h1>About this spec</h1><p class="lede">
        One question: regardless of the platform that sends it, what does a good email do?
        Not a framework, not a guide. A spec: what is required, what is recommended, and
        what to avoid.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <article class="narrow section prose">
    <h2 id="why">Why this exists</h2>
    <p>
      Email is a chain of formats, DNS records, relays, filters, clients, and human
      expectations. The IETF defines the message format, SMTP, and the authentication
      records. W3C publishes the accessibility guidance. Mailbox providers publish their own
      sender requirements, and every client renders HTML its own way. Almost nobody carries
      the whole picture.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site collects those pieces into one provider-agnostic specification of observable
      outcomes. Each topic states what the requirement is, why it matters, how to implement
      it, common mistakes, and how to verify the delivered result.
    </p>

    <h2 id="audience">Who it is for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Engineers</strong> who build or audit the mail an application sends, and want
        one reference.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Email and lifecycle teams</strong> who want to scope quality without inventing
        it from scratch.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Agents</strong> that need a machine-readable description of what to check. See{
          " "
        }
        <a href="/mcp/">MCP</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="what-it-is-not">What it is not</h2>
    <p>
      It is not an ESP comparison, a template framework, legal advice, or a guarantee of
      inbox placement. Standards define the floor; mailbox providers and recipients still
      make their own decisions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status-levels">Status levels</h2>
    <p>
      Every topic has a status. Use them as the default; context can justify exceptions.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Required</strong>: leaving it out breaks delivery, authentication, or access
        for some recipients.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recommended</strong>: what modern mailbox providers, clients, and recipients
        expect.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Optional</strong>: useful in some contexts, not all.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid</strong>: outdated, harmful, or unsafe.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Technologies that were evaluated and left out, such as BIMI and AMP for Email, are
      listed under <a href="/considered/">Considered</a>, with the reason and what would
      change the decision.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-its-built">How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      A static site, generated with Astro. The content is plain Markdown under <code
        >src/content/spec/</code
      >. Sources are part of the schema: a topic without a source does not build.
    </p>

    <h2 id="credits">Credits</h2>
    <p>The spec draws on, and credits on every topic that uses it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/" rel="external">The IETF RFCs</a>: message
        format, MIME, SMTP, SPF, DKIM, DMARC, MTA-STS, DANE, and one-click unsubscribe.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/" rel="external">W3C WAI</a> and WCAG for accessibility.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.caniemail.com/" rel="external">Can I email</a> for client rendering
        support.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.m3aawg.org/" rel="external">M3AAWG</a> and mailbox provider sender
        guidelines for deliverability.
      </li>
      <li>The EU and the UK ICO for privacy and consent; NIST and MITRE for security.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://specification.website/" rel="external">The Website Specification</a
        >, its sibling for the web.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="licensing">Licensing</h2>
    <p>
      Content: <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="external"
        >CC BY 4.0</a
      >. Code: <a href={`${site.repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`} rel="external">MIT</a>. Use it,
      fork it, ship it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Maintained by <a href={site.author.url} rel="external">{site.author.name}</a> and contributors.
      For security reports, follow <a href="/.well-known/security.txt"
        ><code>/.well-known/security.txt</code></a
      >. For anything else, <a href={`${site.repo}/issues`} rel="external">open an issue</a
      >{" "}
      or read how to <a href="/contribute/">contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
